window.docContents[157].resolve({"/Basics/#Logical-Foundations--Basics___-Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Proof-by-Rewriting--Working-with-Numerals":{"contents":"We know from our definitions above that one is just succ zero,\ntwo is succ one, and so on. We can write rules for these equalities too:\n\ntheorem one_eq_succ_zero : one = succ zero := by rfl\ntheorem two_eq_succ_one : two = succ one := by rfl\ntheorem three_eq_succ_two : three = succ two := by rfl\ntheorem four_eq_succ_three : four = succ three := by rfl\n\n\n\n\nFinish the proof using the add rules:\n\nShould this be marked / formatted as an exercise or at least a WORKINCLASS?\nRAB: Let's decide once we choose how to present the laws.\n     My intuition is yes.\n\ntheorem one_plus_one_eq_two : (one + one : Nat) = two := by\n  rewrite [one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\n\nTry the same for two + two = four.\n\ntheorem two_plus_two_eq_four : two + two = four := by\n  sorry\n\n\n\n\n\n\ndef mul (n m : Nat) : Nat :=\n  match m with\n  | zero => zero\n  | succ m' => (mul n m') + n\n\nscoped infixl:70 \" * \" => mul\n\n\nMultiplication, like any function we will prove properties about,\nalso has simplification rules.Remove sorry and prove the simplification rules for mul below.\nYou will likely find the proofs of the simplification rules for add\nto be helpful as a model.@rogerburtonpatel: it would be nice if we could get the\ntheorem statements inside a solution! block as well.theorem mul_zero : ∀ n : Nat, n * zero = zero := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem mul_succ : ∀ n m : Nat, n * (succ m) = (n * m) + n := by\n  sorry\n\nattribute [irreducible] mul\n\n\nProve these thoerems using rewriting with the simplification rules for addition and multiplication.\n\n\n\ntheorem zero_add_one : (zero + one : Nat) = one := by\n  rewrite [one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\ntheorem one_add_one : (one + one : Nat) = two := by\n  rewrite [one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\n\ntheorem zero_mul_two : (zero * two : Nat) = zero := by\n  rewrite [two_eq_succ_one, one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\ntheorem one_mul_two : (one * two : Nat) = two := by\n  rewrite [two_eq_succ_one, one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\ntheorem two_mul_two : (two * two : Nat) = four := by\n  rewrite [two_eq_succ_one, one_eq_succ_zero]\n  sorry\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere is a function beq that tests natural numbers for\nequality, yielding a boolean.\n\ndef beq (n m : Nat) : Bool :=\n  match n with\n  | zero => match m with\n            | zero => true\n            | succ _ => false\n  | succ n' => match m with\n               | zero => false\n               | succ m' => beq n' m'\n\n\nWe could also write this by pattern matching on both n and m at the same time:\n\ndef beq' (n m : Nat) : Bool :=\n  match n, m with\n  | zero, zero => true\n  | zero, succ _ => false\n  | succ _, zero => false\n  | succ n', succ m' => beq n' m'\n\n\nThe definitions of beq and beq' are equivalent.\n\n\n\nSimilarly, the ble function tests whether its first argument is\nless than or equal to its second argument, yielding a boolean.\n\ndef ble (n m : Nat) : Bool :=\n  match n with\n  | zero => true\n  | succ n' =>\n      match m with\n      | zero => false\n      | succ m' => ble n' m'\n\ntheorem zero_ble (n : Nat) : ble zero n = true := by rfl\ntheorem succ_ble_zero (n : Nat) : ble (succ n) zero = false := by rfl\ntheorem succ_ble_succ (n m : Nat) : ble (succ n) (succ m) = ble n m := by rfl\n\nexample : ble two two = true  := by rfl\nexample : ble two four = true  := by rfl\nexample : ble four two = false := by rfl\n\n\n\nDefine a less-than function in terms of ble.def blt (n m : Nat) : Bool\n  := sorry\n\nexample : blt two two = false := sorry\nexample : blt two four = true  := sorry\nexample : blt four two = false := sorry\n\nattribute [irreducible] blt ble\n\n\n\n\nWe'll be using beq a lot, so let's give it an infix notation.\n\nscoped infixl:30 \" == \" => beq\n\n\n\n\nNote that now == and = are different; the former means beq whereas the latter is a logical\nclaim.\n\n\n\ntheorem zero_zero_beq_true : (zero == zero) = true := by rfl\ntheorem zero_succ_beq_false (n : Nat) : (zero == (succ n)) = false := by rfl\ntheorem succ_zero_beq_false (n : Nat) : ((succ n) == zero) = false := by rfl\ntheorem succ_succ_beq (n m : Nat) : ((succ n) == (succ m)) = (n == m) := by rfl\n\nattribute [irreducible] beq\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Basics: Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Proof by Rewriting","header":"1.2.7. Working with Numerals","id":"/Basics/#Logical-Foundations--Basics___-Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Proof-by-Rewriting--Working-with-Numerals"},"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--Logical-Connectives":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Logic in Lean","header":"8.2. Logical Connectives","id":"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--Logical-Connectives"}});